  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Calm Service Reception",
    "overview": "Bayline turns a spoken vehicle-service request into a reviewed appointment. Its white surfaces, slate text, restrained blue controls, and precise dividers convey an orderly reception desk. Crisp system typography is an explicit user-approved choice, with no remote font dependency. Generous space around the call controls balances compact appointment details and conversation rows.\n\nThe product serves fictional callers booking, rescheduling, or cancelling car and SUV services. Every browser owns an isolated two-bay workshop and the fictional Alex Rivera profile. The caller's task stays primary; an expandable inspector exposes the actual Python decisions. No real contact, registration, or payment details belong in this experience.\n\nThis record describes the shipped React interface in `frontend/src/tokens.css`, `frontend/src/styles.css`, and `frontend/src/App.tsx`, checked against the desktop and mobile captures in `artifacts/`. The approved reception overrides in `design-system/bayline/pages/reception.md` take precedence over generic generated recommendations. The reference mock supplies composition, not fictional booking facts to copy.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "White working surfaces on a cool slate canvas.",
      "Restrained blue actions and flat, finely divided panels.",
      "Clear call status, readable conversation, and a prominent appointment time.",
      "Confirmation before changes, with actual decision evidence available on demand."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Action Blue Rule",
        "body": "Use blue to identify an available action or a current proposal; keep most working surfaces white or neutral.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Task First Rule",
        "body": "Keep the call controls and conversation before the appointment ticket in reading order; put technical evidence after the caller's task.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Reception Rule",
        "body": "Separate regions with surface tone, whitespace, and fine dividers rather than ornamental elevation.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** use the recorded blue/slate palette and user-approved local system sans.",
      "**Do** preserve visible focus, meaningful status text, disabled states, and at least 44px primary control targets.",
      "**Do** distinguish a pending proposal from a confirmed appointment and require explicit confirmation.",
      "**Do** render dates, duration, booking ownership, and decision traces from the actual API response.",
      "**Do** keep the mobile composer readable and respect reduced-motion preferences."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** import the generic red CTA or remote-font recommendations over the approved reception overrides.",
      "**Don't** copy invented customer, address, vehicle, duration, or confirmation facts from the mock.",
      "**Don't** replace structural borders with ornamental shadows or turn every detail into a separate card.",
      "**Don't** treat decorative waveform bars as measured audio or imply an LLM made a booking decision.",
      "**Don't** collect real personal, registration, or payment information in this fictional simulation."
    ]
  }
}
